re</source><volume>261</volume><fpage>459</fpage><lpage>467</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">934280</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-May3"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>May</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name><name><surname>Oster</surname><given-names>GF</given-names></name></person-group><year>1976</year><article-title>Bifurcations and dynamic complexity in simple ecological models.</article-title><source>Am Nat</source><volume>110</volume><fpage>573</fpage><lpage>599</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-Hassell1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hassell</surname><given-names>MP</given-names></name><name><surname>Lawton</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>May</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name></person-group><year>1976</year><article-title>Patterns of dynamical behaviour in single-species populations.</article-title><source>J Anim Ecol</source><volume>45</volume><fpage>471</fpage><lpage>486</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-Berryman1"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berryman</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Millstein</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><year>1989</year><article-title>Are ecological systems chaotic - and if not, why not?</article-title><source>Trends Ecol Evol</source><volume>4</volume><fpage>26</fpage><lpage>28</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-Caswell1"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Caswell</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Weeks</surname><given-names>DE</given-names></name></person-group><year>1986</year><article-title>Two-sex models - chaos, extinction, and other dynamic consequences of sex.</article-title><source>Am Nat</source><volume>128</volume><fpage>707</fpage><lpage>735</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-Allen1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Allen</surname><given-names>KR</given-names></name></person-group><year>1980</year><article-title>Conservation and management of whales.</article-title><publisher-loc>Seattle</publisher-loc><publisher-name>University of Washington Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-Sherman1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sherman</surname><given-names>PW</given-names></name><name><surname>Morton</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name></person-group><year>1984</year><article-title>Demography of belding ground squirrels.</article-title><source>Ecology</source><volume>65</volume><fpage>1617</fpage><lpage>1628</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-CluttonBrock1"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Clutton-Brock</surname><given-names>TH</given-names></name></person-group><year>1991</year><article-title>The evolution of parental care.</article-title><publisher-loc>Princeton</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Princeton University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000624-Massot1"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Massot</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Clobert</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Pilorge</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Lecomte</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Barbault</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>1992</year><article-title>Density dependence in the common lizard - demographic consequences of a density manipulation.</article-